## IMPLEMENTATION

### 1. Update the status bar config in the root layout
In `app/_layout.tsx`, change the existing `<StatusBar>` component (from `expo-status-bar`) from its current translucent/light setup to fully hidden:
```tsx
<StatusBar hidden={true} />
```
This should apply globally across all screens since it's set once in the root layout — remove any per-screen status bar overrides if they exist elsewhere (e.g., if the Camera screen or any other screen sets its own `<StatusBar>` config, delete that and let the root layout's `hidden={true}` apply everywhere consistently).

### 2. Android: combine with the existing immersive nav bar behavior
Since the nav bar is already using `NavigationBar.setBehaviorAsync('overlay-swipe')` (swipe-to-reveal, then auto-hide again) from the earlier immersive setup, make the status bar behave the same way for consistency — a fully hidden status bar with no way to reveal it can feel broken/glitchy to some users (e.g., they can't check the time or see if a call is coming in). Implement this using Android's system UI visibility flags so BOTH bars hide together and BOTH can be swiped-to-reveal temporarily from either edge, then auto-hide again — rather than one being swipe-revealable and the other being permanently invisible with no way to check it.
- If `expo-status-bar`'s simple `hidden` prop doesn't support this swipe-reveal behavior on its own (it's a simpler API than the full native immersive mode), use `expo-navigation-bar`'s existing immersive setup as the single source of truth for BOTH bars together: set the same `'overlay-swipe'` behavior config to affect both system bars simultaneously (Android's edge-to-edge/immersive APIs are typically controlled together at the OS level, not independently per-bar) — check `expo-navigation-bar`'s and `expo-status-bar`'s current documentation/API for the correct combined approach given the installed Expo SDK version, since this exact API can differ between SDK versions.
- If truly independent control isn't cleanly available, prioritize matching the existing nav bar's swipe-reveal-then-rehide behavior over a permanently-invisible status bar, so the user experience is consistent between the two bars.

### 3. iOS: hide the status bar consistently too
- Confirm `<StatusBar hidden={true} />` from `expo-status-bar` also correctly hides it on iOS (this is a simpler, more universally supported API on iOS than Android's immersive mode nuances above).
- If any screen uses a native modal, camera view, or full-screen presentation that manages its own status bar visibility independently (this can happen with certain camera/video components), make sure that per-screen behavior doesn't override the global hidden setting — audit the Camera screen specifically since it's the most likely place a native component might reassert its own status bar behavior.

### 4. Safe area handling — important, don't skip
Since the status bar is now hidden everywhere, re-check every screen that previously relied on `useSafeAreaInsets()` top inset to avoid overlapping the status bar area. With the status bar hidden, content can often extend further up than before — decide intentionally, per screen, whether content should now use that reclaimed space (e.g., Dashboard header can sit slightly higher) or whether you still want a consistent top padding for visual balance even without a visible status bar. Don't leave awkward empty gaps at the top of screens that were previously sized around a visible status bar that no longer exists.

